# Watch policies -- YOUR repository's named watch sets. Shipped with none
# defined, deliberately: which files a claim's evidence reads is a fact
# about YOUR tree, and a template that guessed would be the freehand
# problem one level up.
#
# FORMAT, one policy per line:
#     <policy-name> -- <glob>[, <glob>...]
# Names are lowercase [a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*. '#' comments and blanks are
# ignored. Pathspec magic (a line or glob starting ':', '-', '!') is
# REFUSED: these are CLI globs handed to match_paths(), never git
# pathspecs (SI-1, the same rule .truth/citation-scope carries).
#
#     scripts/truth claim "..." --watch-policy <name> --evidence-cmd ...
#
# --watch-policy and --paths cannot be combined: a claim's watch set comes
# from ONE source, or which one wins would depend on argument order.
#
# THIS FILE IS OPTIONAL AND ITS EMPTINESS IS NOT A FINDING. Unlike
# .truth/generated-paths and .truth/citation-scope, an absent or empty
# watch-policies file configures nothing away -- claims simply pass
# --paths by hand, exactly as they did before this feature existed. So
# there is no attestation line to write here and no doctor row to satisfy:
# an unused feature must not redden your installation check. `truth stats`
# reports adoption as a number, which is the honest treatment of an opt-in.
#
# WHY YOU MIGHT WANT THEM. Every claim picking its own path list means
# nothing checks that the list has anything to do with the evidence, and
# the drift runs both ways: watching a file the recipe never opens (noise
# on every edit) and missing one it does (a claim nobody is warned about).
# In the template's own repository, 75 claims with a watch set held 60
# DISTINCT sets -- near-zero reuse -- and a single edit to the busiest
# watched file named 31 claims in the pre-edit whisper.
#
# The rule that makes a policy correct is one line, and it is worth
# writing on the wall: WATCH EXACTLY THE PATHS THE RECIPE READS. Not the
# docs the claim is about; not the ADR that motivated it. The paths the
# evidence command opens.
#
# SUB-TREE TARGETS. A glob may name part of a file instead of the whole
# of it, by appending '#<selector>' -- for .json, .toml and .md only:
#
#   stripe-dep     -- package.json#/dependencies/stripe
#   lint-config    -- pyproject.toml#tool.ruff.lint
#   jwt-section    -- docs/spec.md#2-session-management
#
# A '#' at the START of a line is still a comment; only a mid-glob '#'
# is a selector. JSON/TOML take an RFC 6901 pointer (/a/b) or a dot-path
# (a.b); Markdown takes a heading title or its anchor slug.
#
# WHAT IT BUYS: `truth reproduce` compares the DIGEST of that sub-tree at
# two commits, so a dependency bump three keys over is not an event and
# never reports the claim as drifted. Selector targets are also outside
# the one-path freehand budget and the churn floor, because the author
# named an exact address and intake read the file to confirm it resolves.
#
# WHEN NOT TO. The rule above still governs: watch what the recipe READS.
# A recipe that greps a whole document for four phrases reads the whole
# document, and anchoring it to one section would make the watch narrower
# than the evidence -- the same drift as watching a file the recipe never
# opens, pointed the other way. Selectors pay when the recipe's subject
# genuinely IS one key or one section, which is most often a config file
# a dependency or a version lives in.
#
# EXAMPLES -- delete these comments and write your own:
#
#   core-suite     -- tests/test_core.py
#   api-surface    -- src/api/*.py, tests/test_api.py
#   build-config   -- pyproject.toml, Makefile
